"── 106_winui_editorpane.mst — WG6c-1: the pane paints ───────────────────
 docs/sprints/sprint_wg6c_detail.md WG6c-1. A code pane we draw ourselves,
 over the rope that already exists.

 WHY OURS RATHER THAN RICHEDIT: the model half was already built and tested
 (`world/60_editor.mst` — a persistent rope with a newline index and free
 undo, pinned against a String oracle) and so was the highlighter (WG5c
 D1/D2, deliberately control-agnostic). What was missing was the view. The
 sprint records the full reasoning; the short version is that building the
 Editor on RichEdit and then replacing it would be writing it twice.

 WHY A CHILD OF OUR OWN WINDOW CLASS: an SS_OWNERDRAW STATIC would paint
 through the WM_DRAWITEM path WG4 D3 already built, and it can even hold
 focus (measured: SetFocus on a STATIC works and GetFocus answers it) — but
 a STATIC's WndProc is the SYSTEM's, and only WM_COMMAND/WM_NOTIFY/
 WM_DRAWITEM are forwarded to a parent. WM_KEYDOWN and WM_CHAR are not, so
 the pane would be focusable and mute. A child of `WinShell className` has
 the DOOR as its WndProc, and both key messages are already allowlisted.

 THE VIEW OWNS NO TEXT. `EditorSession` holds the document and answers the
 whole buffer plus a caret offset on every change; this file paints what it
 is given. 60's own note records that the earlier incremental line-damage
 protocol DID drift, and that blasting the whole buffer replaced it — so
 there is no line bookkeeping here to fall out of sync, by construction.

 MONOSPACED, WHICH IS WHY THE ARITHMETIC IS EASY: with a fixed advance,
 column N is at `N * charWidth` and line M at `M * lineHeight`. No per-run
 measurement, no cumulative rounding, and hit-testing (WG6c-2) is a
 division."

Object subclass: WinApi [

    WinApi class >> createCaret: hwnd <Integer> bitmap: hbm <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateCaret ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'CreateCaret'
    ]
    WinApi class >> setCaretPos: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetCaretPos ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'SetCaretPos'
    ]
    WinApi class >> showCaret: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #ShowCaret ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'ShowCaret'
    ]
    WinApi class >> hideCaret: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #HideCaret ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'HideCaret'
    ]
    WinApi class >> destroyCaret ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #DestroyCaret ret: #g args: #()>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'DestroyCaret'
    ]

    "WG6c-2c: THE CELL GRID. `ExtTextOutW`'s last argument is an array of
     per-character advances, which is what makes this a grid of cells rather
     than a string GDI is free to lay out however the font asks.

     That distinction was the bug. `DrawText` lays a run out using the FONT's
     own advances; this file then positioned each run on its own
     `column * charWidth` grid. The two agree only if the measured advance is
     exactly the rendered one, and it is not — GDI rounds per glyph — so the
     glyphs inside a run drifted, the next run snapped back to the grid, and
     the overlap read on screen as doubled letters every few characters. It
     was reported, correctly, as `its bollocks`.

     With an explicit dx array every glyph lands on its own cell by
     construction and no amount of rounding can accumulate."
    WinApi class >> extTextOut: hdc <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                    options: opts <Integer> rect: r <Integer>
                    text: s <Integer> count: n <Integer> dx: dx <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #ExtTextOutW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'ExtTextOutW'
    ]

    "WG6c-2c: a DC to measure in, with no window and no paint. `GetDC(NULL)`
     answers one for the whole screen — the conventional way to ask GDI about
     a font's metrics when you are not drawing — and it must be released."
    WinApi class >> getTextExtentPoint32: hdc <Integer> text: str <Integer>
                    count: n <Integer> into: sizePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "The font's OWN width for a string — what GDI would lay it out as if
         nobody handed it a grid. Present so the drift between that and
         `units * charWidth` is a MEASURED NUMBER rather than an assumption;
         see `editorGlyphDrift`."
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetTextExtentPoint32W ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'GetTextExtentPoint32W'
    ]

    WinApi class >> getDC: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetDC ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'GetDC'
    ]
    WinApi class >> releaseDC: hwnd <Integer> dc: hdc <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #ReleaseDC ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'ReleaseDC'
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: EditorPaneHwnd EditorCharW EditorLineH PaintCalls PaintError CaretOwned MetricsError PaintOriginX PaintOriginY LastPaintRect SegDraws EditorDx EditorDxWidth SizeScratch MetricsDpi>

    WinShell class >> editorPaneName ^ <Symbol> [ ^#editorPane ]

    "── The pane ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> buildEditorPane [
        "Created with OUR class name, which is what puts the door in front of
         its keystrokes. Every other control in this shell is a stock class
         and paints itself; this one is ours and does not."
        | c |
        (self controlNamed: self editorPaneName) ifNotNil: [ :x | ^self ].
        c := self
            addControl: self editorPaneName
            class: self className
            text: ''
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_CLIPSIBLINGS'))
            exStyle: 0.
        c ifNotNil: [ :x | EditorPaneHwnd := x handle ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorPaneHwnd ^ <Integer> [
        ^EditorPaneHwnd isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ EditorPaneHwnd ] ]

    "── Metrics, measured once ───────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> ensureEditorMetrics [
        "One character of a MONOSPACED face, measured ONCE, OFF SCREEN, and
         NOT during a paint.

         `DT_CALCRECT` computes and draws nothing — it never did draw — but
         this used to run off the PAINT's own DC, which made the metrics
         depend on a paint having happened. They answer defaults until then and
         change exactly once, so anything doing pixel arithmetic before the
         first paint used 8 and anything after used the real 7. WG6c-2's gate
         caught it the hard way: a click encoded by one control-port call and
         decoded by a later one straddled the transition and landed at the end
         of the document instead of on line 1 column 4. Measuring here closes
         that at the source rather than making every caller order itself
         around a paint.

         `GetDC(NULL)` is a DC for the whole screen — the conventional thing to
         measure against when you are not drawing — and it is RELEASED on every
         path including a raise, for the reason `BeginPaint`/`EndPaint` and
         `OpenClipboard`/`CloseClipboard` are: a leaked DC is a process-wide
         resource that nothing here would notice losing.

         A failure leaves the defaults in place rather than propagating. Off
         Windows there is no GDI at all, and the arithmetic above is checked
         against `editorCharWidth` rather than against 8 — so a world with no
         metrics still computes consistently, it just computes in units of a
         font it could not ask about."
        | dc target |
        (EditorCharW isNil not and: [ MetricsDpi = self editorMetricsDpi ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "THE PANE'S OWN DC when there is a pane, so the measurement happens in
         the same device context the drawing will. `GetDC(NULL)` is the
         fallback for the window-less case (a test, a headless load) and is
         the PRIMARY monitor's context — which is the right answer only while
         there is one monitor at one scale.

         That distinction is the whole of this defect's remaining risk. The
         glyphs are placed `charWidth` apart by the dx grid, so a charWidth
         measured against a DIFFERENT font size than the one selected when
         drawing does not merely misalign — it makes the glyphs OVERLAP, which
         reads as smeared, doubled letters. Measuring the same font object in
         the same DC makes that impossible rather than unlikely, and keying the
         cache on DPI means moving the window to a differently-scaled monitor
         re-measures instead of keeping a stale number."
        target := self editorPaneHwnd.
        [ dc := WinApi getDC: target.
          dc = 0 ifFalse: [
              [ | old rect |
                old := WinApi selectObject: dc object: self monoFont.
                rect := self drawRectScratch.
                self setRect: rect x: 0 y: 0 w: 4096 h: 256.
                WinApi drawText: dc
                    text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: 'M') count: 0 - 1
                    rect: rect
                    format: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_CALCRECT')
                        bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_LEFT') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'DT_SINGLELINE'))).
                EditorCharW := ((WinArena rectAt: rect) at: 3) max: 1.
                EditorLineH := ((WinArena rectAt: rect) at: 4) max: 1.
                WinApi selectObject: dc object: old.
                MetricsDpi := self editorMetricsDpi
              ] ensure: [ WinApi releaseDC: target dc: dc ] ]
        ] on: Error do: [ :e | MetricsError := e messageText ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMetricsDpi ^ <Integer> [
        "The DPI the metrics were taken at. `Dpi` is nil until the window
         opens, and `monoFont` bakes it in at creation — so a metric taken
         before the window exists describes a different font from the one that
         will be drawn with. Keying on it turns that from a silent mismatch
         into a re-measurement."
        ^self dpi isNil ifTrue: [ 96 ] ifFalse: [ self dpi ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMetrics ^ <Array> [
        "Everything a reader needs to tell a good metric from a stale one:
         #(dpi charWidth lineHeight naturalWidthOfTenChars drift). Reported by
         the gate, and the first thing to ask for when the pane looks smeared."
        ^Array
            with: self editorMetricsDpi
            with: self editorCharWidth
            with: self editorLineHeight
            with: (self naturalWidthOf: 'MMMMMMMMMM')
            with: (self editorGlyphDrift: 'MMMMMMMMMM')
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMaxCells ^ <Integer> [ ^2048 ]

    WinShell class >> utf16UnitsOf: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "UTF-16 CODE UNITS, which is what `ExtTextOutW` counts — and NOT the
         same number as `aString size`. A guest String is UTF-8 in this
         dialect, so its size is a BYTE count; the two agree for ASCII and
         part company at the first accented character. `caf` + U+00E9 is five
         bytes and four units.

         This matters because `ExtTextOutW` takes an explicit count where
         `DrawText` took -1 and found its own terminator. Handing it the byte
         count made it read PAST the NUL — one glyph of whatever followed the
         string in the arena. Found by measuring the two numbers rather than
         by reasoning about them, which is the only way this kind of thing
         gets found.

         The COLUMN arithmetic elsewhere in this file still counts in the
         guest String's own units, which is the boundary rule WG5a and WG5c D4
         both record and which this release scopes out with CJK. A non-ASCII
         character therefore still advances the column by more cells than it
         occupies; that is a known, named gap, not a new one."
        ^(aString asUtf16Alien size // 2) - 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorDxBuffer ^ <Integer> [
        "ONE ADVANCE PER CELL, all of them identical — the grid, as a block of
         memory GDI reads. Built once and rebuilt only if the measured advance
         changes, which it does exactly once (never, after
         `ensureEditorMetrics`).

         This is the whole of what makes the pane a character grid rather than
         a string GDI lays out for us, and it is the smallest form of the
         obvious next step: the guest poking cells into a buffer and the
         renderer displaying them. Today the cells are advances and the
         characters still travel as a string; the natural follow-on is a
         char+colour buffer per cell, which would also take the tokenizer pass
         out of the paint handler entirely."
        | w |
        w := self editorCharWidth.
        (EditorDx isNil or: [ EditorDxWidth ~= w ]) ifTrue: [
            EditorDx isNil ifTrue: [ EditorDx := WinArena alloc: 4 * self editorMaxCells ].
            0 to: self editorMaxCells - 1 do: [ :i |
                WinArena u32At: EditorDx + (i * 4) put: w ].
            EditorDxWidth := w ].
        ^EditorDx
    ]

    WinShell class >> naturalWidthOf: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "What GDI would make this string, laying it out its own way. Measured
         in a screen DC with the code font selected — the same DC
         `ensureEditorMetrics` uses and for the same reason: no window, no
         paint, no dependency on when the first repaint happened.

         Answers 0 when it cannot measure (off Windows, no GDI), which callers
         read as `no drift information` rather than as `no drift`."
        | dc out |
        out := 0.
        [ dc := WinApi getDC: 0.
          dc = 0 ifFalse: [
              [ | old sz |
                old := WinApi selectObject: dc object: self monoFont.
                sz := self sizeScratch.
                WinApi getTextExtentPoint32: dc
                    text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: aString)
                    count: (self utf16UnitsOf: aString)
                    into: sz.
                out := WinArena i32At: sz.
                WinApi selectObject: dc object: old
              ] ensure: [ WinApi releaseDC: 0 dc: dc ] ]
        ] on: Error do: [ :e | out := 0 ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinShell class >> sizeScratch ^ <Integer> [
        "Eight bytes: a SIZE is two LONGs. Allocated once and reused, like
         every other scratch block in this layer."
        SizeScratch isNil ifTrue: [ SizeScratch := WinArena alloc: 8 ].
        ^SizeScratch
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorGlyphDrift: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "HOW FAR THE FONT AND THE GRID DISAGREE over one string, in pixels.

         This is the bug that shipped, as a number. The pane positions each
         syntax run at `column * charWidth` but used to hand the run's TEXT to
         `DrawText`, which lays it out using the font's own per-glyph advances.
         Non-zero drift means the glyphs inside a run walk away from the grid
         and the next run snaps back — which on screen is doubled letters every
         few characters, and was reported as exactly that.

         `ExtTextOutW` with an explicit advance array makes the drift
         IRRELEVANT rather than zero, which is why this is reported rather than
         asserted to be zero: the number is expected to be non-zero, and its
         being non-zero is precisely why the dx array is load-bearing. A future
         change back to `DrawText` would be reintroducing this defect, and this
         is here so that is visible instead of subtle."
        | natural |
        natural := self naturalWidthOf: aString.
        natural = 0 ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^natural - ((self utf16UnitsOf: aString) * self editorCharWidth)
    ]

    WinShell class >> metricsError ^ <String> [
        ^MetricsError isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ MetricsError ] ]

    WinShell class >> editorCharWidth ^ <Integer> [
        self ensureEditorMetrics.
        ^EditorCharW isNil ifTrue: [ 8 ] ifFalse: [ EditorCharW ] ]
    WinShell class >> editorLineHeight ^ <Integer> [
        self ensureEditorMetrics.
        ^EditorLineH isNil ifTrue: [ 16 ] ifFalse: [ EditorLineH ] ]

    "── The paint, called SYNCHRONOUSLY from the door ────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> paintWindow: hwnd <Integer> dc: hdc <Integer>
                      x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "WM_PAINT, between the door's BeginPaint and EndPaint. Answers 1 when
         it painted and 0 to let DefWindowProcW have it.

         A RAISE IN HERE IS INVISIBLE BY NATURE — the pane simply stays blank
         and nothing says why — so it is captured into `PaintError` rather
         than propagated, exactly as WG4 D3's own drawing handler does. The
         door's EndPaint runs either way; that is deliberately not this
         method's responsibility."
        PaintCalls := (PaintCalls isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ PaintCalls ]) + 1.
        hwnd = self editorPaneHwnd ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self paintEditorIn: hdc x: x y: y w: w h: h ]
            on: Error do: [ :e | PaintError := e messageText. 0 ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> paintCalls ^ <Integer> [
        ^PaintCalls isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ PaintCalls ] ]
    WinShell class >> paintError ^ <String> [
        ^PaintError isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ PaintError ] ]

    WinShell class >> editorText ^ <String> [
        "Whatever the session holds, or an empty document. The view asks; it
         never keeps a copy — which is the property that makes it impossible
         for the two to disagree."
        ^EditorSession current
            ifNil: [ '' ]
            ifNotNil: [ :s | s documentString ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> paintEditorIn: hdc <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                      w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | rect brush old text runs lineH charW lineIndex lineStart |
        "THE DOCUMENT IS DRAWN IN CLIENT COORDINATES. `x`/`y` are rcPaint —
         the INVALID rectangle — and they belong to the ERASE below and to
         nothing else.

         This drew the text at `x + column * charW`, which is correct only
         when the invalid rectangle happens to start at the client origin. It
         always does when we invalidate ourselves (`refreshEditorPane` passes
         NULL, meaning the whole client), which is why every gate and every
         screenshot looked right. It does NOT when Windows invalidates a PART
         of the pane — another window dragged across it, a partial uncover, a
         resize — and then the whole document was redrawn displaced by that
         rectangle's origin, on top of the copy already there. Reported from
         the screen, where it is obvious, by someone using the thing.

         The caret never had the bug (`SetCaretPos` is client-relative), which
         is precisely what makes the artifact read as `the text is drawn
         twice, offset` rather than as `the pane is in the wrong place`."
        PaintOriginX := 0.
        PaintOriginY := 0.
        "Recorded so a gate can prove it exercised a PARTIAL paint — the shape
         `refreshEditorPane` cannot produce, because it invalidates with NULL."
        LastPaintRect := Array with: x with: y with: w with: h.
        SegDraws := 0.
        rect := self drawRectScratch.
        self setRect: rect x: x y: y w: w h: h.
        brush := WinApi createSolidBrush: 16rFFFFFF.
        WinApi fillRect: hdc rect: rect brush: brush.
        WinApi deleteObject: brush.
        old := WinApi selectObject: hdc object: self monoFont.
        WinApi setBkMode: hdc mode: (WinApi constant: 'TRANSPARENT').
        text := self editorText.
        text isEmpty ifTrue: [
            WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old.
            ^1 ].
        "WG6c-2b: THE SELECTION, UNDER THE TEXT. Filled before a single glyph
         is drawn, and the text then drawn over it in TRANSPARENT mode — which
         is why the syntax colours survive the highlight instead of being
         replaced by a system highlight colour. A code editor that loses its
         colouring the moment you select something has traded one useful thing
         for another.

         The rectangles are computed in 107, purely, so the arithmetic that
         decides what looks selected is checkable without a window — the same
         reason `caretPointFor:` lives outside the paint. Its cost is nothing
         when there is no selection: the collection is empty and this loop
         does not run."
        (self editorSelectionRectsAtX: PaintOriginX y: PaintOriginY) do: [ :sr |
            | sb |
            sb := WinApi createSolidBrush: 16rF0D8A8.   "a light blue, BGR"
            self setRect: rect x: (sr at: 1) y: (sr at: 2) w: (sr at: 3) h: (sr at: 4).
            WinApi fillRect: hdc rect: rect brush: sb.
            WinApi deleteObject: sb ].
        runs := WinSyntax runsFor: text.
        lineH := self editorLineHeight.
        charW := self editorCharWidth.
        lineIndex := 0.
        lineStart := 1.
        "One pass over the tiling runs, splitting each at newlines. The runs
         TILE the source (66's backbone property), so walking them walks every
         character exactly once and the column counter cannot drift."
        runs do: [ :r |
            | s e i seg |
            s := r at: 1.
            e := s + (r at: 2) - 1.
            i := s.
            [ i <= e ] whileTrue: [
                | j |
                j := i.
                [ j <= e and: [ (text at: j) ~= Character nl ] ] whileTrue: [ j := j + 1 ].
                j > i ifTrue: [
                    seg := text copyFrom: i to: j - 1.
                    WinApi setTextColor: hdc color: (WinSyntax colorFor: (r at: 3)).
                    SegDraws := SegDraws + 1.
                    "ExtTextOutW with the dx grid, NOT DrawText — every glyph
                     pinned to its own cell. The count is in UTF-16 UNITS; see
                     `utf16UnitsOf:` for why that is not the String's size."
                    WinApi extTextOut: hdc
                        x: PaintOriginX + ((i - lineStart) * charW)
                        y: PaintOriginY + (lineIndex * lineH)
                        options: 0
                        rect: 0
                        text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: seg)
                        count: ((self utf16UnitsOf: seg) min: self editorMaxCells)
                        dx: self editorDxBuffer ].
                (j <= e and: [ (text at: j) = Character nl ]) ifTrue: [
                    lineIndex := lineIndex + 1.
                    lineStart := j + 1 ].
                i := j + 1 ] ].
        WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old.
        ^1
    ]

    "── The caret ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> caretPointFor: offset <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "Offset (1-based, as the rope counts) to a pixel position. PURE over
         the text, so the arithmetic that puts the cursor in the right place
         is checkable without a window — and it is the same arithmetic
         WG6c-2's hit-testing will invert."
        | text line col i |
        text := self editorText.
        line := 0.
        col := 0.
        i := 1.
        [ i < offset and: [ i <= text size ] ] whileTrue: [
            (text at: i) = Character nl
                ifTrue: [ line := line + 1. col := 0 ]
                ifFalse: [ col := col + 1 ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^Array with: col * self editorCharWidth with: line * self editorLineHeight
    ]

    WinShell class >> syncEditorCaret [
        "Created on focus, destroyed on losing it. The caret is a THREAD-wide
         resource in Win32, not a per-window one: CreateCaret replaces
         whatever caret exists anywhere, so a pane that made one and never
         destroyed it leaves a cursor blinking in a window that does not have
         focus — which reads as two cursors and is a classic."
        | hwnd p |
        hwnd := self editorPaneHwnd.
        hwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        WinApi getFocus = hwnd
            ifFalse: [
                CaretOwned == true ifTrue: [ WinApi destroyCaret. CaretOwned := false ].
                ^self ].
        CaretOwned == true ifFalse: [
            WinApi createCaret: hwnd bitmap: 0 width: 2 height: self editorLineHeight.
            WinApi showCaret: hwnd.
            CaretOwned := true ].
        "THE VIEW'S caret, not the session's. 107's header states the rule —
         the view owns the caret for NAVIGATION and the session owns it only
         across an EDIT — and this line read the session's, so every arrow
         key and every click moved a caret that was then drawn back at the
         last edit position. The `two authorities would drift` hazard, in the
         file that wrote it down. `editorCaret` is the view's, and after an
         edit it has already adopted the session's answer."
        p := self caretPointFor: self editorCaret.
        WinApi setCaretPos: (p at: 1) + 2 y: (p at: 2) + 2.
        ^self
    ]

    "── Layout and refresh ───────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> layoutEditorPane: content <Array> [
        (self controlNamed: self editorPaneName) ifNotNil: [ :c | c moveTo: content ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> segDraws ^ <Integer> [
        "How many DrawText calls the LAST paint made. One per coloured run per
         line — 31 for the demo class, which the headless simulation in
         `/tmp` and this counter must agree on. They disagree if anything
         iterates the document twice, which is what drawing the same glyphs at
         two slightly different pitches looks like on screen."
        ^SegDraws isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ SegDraws ] ]

    WinShell class >> lastPaintRect ^ <Array> [
        ^LastPaintRect isNil ifTrue: [ #(0 0 0 0) ] ifFalse: [ LastPaintRect ] ]

    WinShell class >> invalidateEditorRectX: rx <Integer> y: ry <Integer> w: rw <Integer> h: rh <Integer> [
        "A PARTIAL invalidation, which is what Windows does when another window
         is dragged across the pane and what `refreshEditorPane` never does —
         it passes NULL, meaning the whole client. That difference is exactly
         why the paint's coordinate bug survived every gate and every
         screenshot and was found by looking at the running app. Present so a
         gate can exercise the shape deliberately."
        | r |
        self editorPaneHwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        r := self drawRectScratch.
        self setRect: r x: rx y: ry w: rw h: rh.
        WinApi invalidateRect: self editorPaneHwnd rect: r erase: 0.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> refreshEditorPane [
        "InvalidateRect rather than a direct draw, for WG4 D3's own reason: a
         caller is usually inside a VM entry holding the busy guard, and a
         paint provoked synchronously from there would be declined at the
         door. Invalidation is asynchronous and arrives through the pump."
        self editorPaneHwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        WinApi invalidateRect: self editorPaneHwnd rect: 0 erase: 0.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> openEditorOn: aString <String> [
        EditorSession openOn: aString.
        "The VIEW's caret is the view's to reset, and nothing else was doing
         it: the session starts a new document at 1 while the view kept the
         previous class's caret and selection, which on a shorter document ran
         off the end. A no-op until 107 defines it."
        self resetEditorCaret.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        ^self
    ]
]
